WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:04.679
Now, let's dive into actually going hands-on and creating

2
00:00:04.679 --> 00:00:07.660
our camera cuts in our sequence.

3
00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:10.140
Now, if you haven't already, go into your Content Browser.

4
00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:13.740
And under the Sequence 105.0.0 folder,

5
00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:15.300
go ahead and create a new folder

6
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:16.660
and name it whatever you'd like.

7
00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:22.059
But in this case, we'll do SEQ underscore EX, for example.

8
00:00:22.059 --> 00:00:24.260
And then from within that Content Browser,

9
00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:26.820
or if you want to create it from the main toolbar,

10
00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:30.019
go ahead and create your level sequence by right-clicking

11
00:00:30.019 --> 00:00:33.140
and under the Cinematics dropdown,

12
00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:35.200
we will create a level sequence.

13
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.380
And you can name it SEQ 105 underscore EX, for example,

14
00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:42.530
or whatever is easiest for you.

15
00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:44.310
All right, and if you haven't already,

16
00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:47.329
in your Content Browser, make sure that you have the Sequence

17
00:00:47.329 --> 00:00:51.170
105 start map open, which will be in the Sequence 105

18
00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:52.729
folder under Maps.

19
00:00:52.729 --> 00:00:55.369
Go ahead and open that up, and we'll get started.

20
00:00:55.369 --> 00:00:58.210
So I'm going to fly up to this little platform

21
00:00:58.210 --> 00:00:59.170
that we have up here.

22
00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:03.170
And this is where we're going to create our first animation.

23
00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:05.890
Now, if you have one of the existing sequences in there,

24
00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:06.530
not a problem.

25
00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:07.609
Go ahead and just delete it.

26
00:01:07.609 --> 00:01:09.230
Again, that won't delete the sequence.

27
00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:12.829
That'll just delete the actor in the level.

28
00:01:12.829 --> 00:01:15.790
All right, so the story that I want to build here

29
00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:18.150
is that, let's say that I'm playing in game

30
00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:20.049
and I trip a cut scene.

31
00:01:20.049 --> 00:01:22.030
And in that, I've got this special character

32
00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:24.870
that runs from this platform, jumps down,

33
00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:26.930
comes galloping across the landscape,

34
00:01:26.969 --> 00:01:30.650
and comes to my location to give me a quest.

35
00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:33.170
So that's the story of the narrative that I'm building.

36
00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:35.609
And I'm going to do that in three shots.

37
00:01:35.609 --> 00:01:37.109
So for this one, we're actually

38
00:01:37.109 --> 00:01:39.290
just going to set up the first shot,

39
00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:42.760
and then we'll cover how you can create the other two.

40
00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:45.959
All right, so I'm going to go down to my Content Drawer.

41
00:01:45.959 --> 00:01:48.280
And in my Sequence Example, I'm going

42
00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:50.519
to open up that sequence that we just created.

43
00:01:50.519 --> 00:01:52.879
Now, mine just says Example here.

44
00:01:52.879 --> 00:01:54.120
You may have the other one.

45
00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.579
Whichever one you created, just go ahead and double click.

46
00:01:56.659 --> 00:02:00.180
Let's open it, and now we have our blank sequence.

47
00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:01.739
OK, so the first thing that I'll do

48
00:02:01.739 --> 00:02:04.620
is actually just stage our actor in this scene.

49
00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:07.099
So I'm going to go back to my Content Browser,

50
00:02:07.099 --> 00:02:09.900
and I'm going to go to the Stacobot folder.

51
00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:14.020
And I believe what I'm looking for is under Characters, Bot,

52
00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:18.979
and it should be, find it here, there it is.

53
00:02:18.979 --> 00:02:23.580
Under our Meshes, we're going to use the skm underscore bot.

54
00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:26.419
And you'll notice the little purple light pink bar,

55
00:02:26.419 --> 00:02:28.340
and it says Skeletal Mesh.

56
00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:29.580
This is what we want.

57
00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.580
Let's go ahead and just drag them into the scene,

58
00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:34.110
put them anywhere is fine.

59
00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:36.370
I'm going to jump back to my sequence,

60
00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:39.310
and what I'm going to do is going to right click,

61
00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:41.469
and I'm going to do this Actor to Sequence.

62
00:02:41.469 --> 00:02:44.069
And you'll notice that because I have the bot selected

63
00:02:44.069 --> 00:02:48.110
in the world, the Add SKM Bot is at the top.

64
00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:51.030
Now, if the actor wasn't selected,

65
00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:53.430
I've deselected it now, and I right click Actor to Sequence,

66
00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:56.830
you'll notice that I'd have to go in and search from my bar.

67
00:02:57.789 --> 00:02:59.689
Click our actor, make this easy on ourselves,

68
00:02:59.689 --> 00:03:03.319
right click, Actor to Sequence, SKM Bot.

69
00:03:03.319 --> 00:03:05.860
And there we go, so now we have our actor in the sequence.

70
00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:08.719
Now, we are also going to convert this over

71
00:03:08.719 --> 00:03:10.560
to a replaceable.

72
00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:12.599
As we covered in one of the earlier videos,

73
00:03:12.599 --> 00:03:15.560
this is a new type of actor reference

74
00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:17.120
that we can have inside of our sequence,

75
00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:20.639
meaning that we can replace this character at runtime,

76
00:03:20.639 --> 00:03:22.800
but for the purposes of building our sequence,

77
00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:24.979
this is the example we can work with.

78
00:03:25.219 --> 00:03:27.080
I'll go ahead and just do Convert Selected Bindings

79
00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:31.069
to Replaceable Actor, and notice the icon changes,

80
00:03:31.069 --> 00:03:33.300
and we are good to go.

81
00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:34.680
All right, now the first thing that we're gonna do

82
00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.479
is animate this character running off

83
00:03:37.479 --> 00:03:39.360
of our little platform here.

84
00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:41.000
So I'll just go ahead and move him back,

85
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:43.280
and I'll put on my rotation.

86
00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:46.360
Now, if you don't want snapping, just go up to your main,

87
00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:48.400
kind of the little bar up here in the upper right,

88
00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:50.080
and you can turn off snapping.

89
00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:52.240
So I'll turn off the rotation,

90
00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:53.960
and I'll turn off my grid snap.

91
00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:56.599
So now I can do just some fine adjustments,

92
00:03:56.599 --> 00:03:57.439
and that's perfect.

93
00:03:57.439 --> 00:04:01.169
So we'll make sure he's lined up, ready to go.

94
00:04:01.169 --> 00:04:04.919
And now what I'm gonna do underneath my SKM bot,

95
00:04:04.919 --> 00:04:07.680
under Animations, I'm gonna click our little plus button,

96
00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:10.620
and I'm just gonna type in run,

97
00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:13.680
and that should give us our A bot run.

98
00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.099
We'll go ahead and add this animation,

99
00:04:15.099 --> 00:04:17.120
and you'll see if I scrub this,

100
00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:19.680
we get one of our animation loops.

101
00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:23.000
So let's go ahead and just drag this animation out to,

102
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:27.040
I don't know, something around like maybe 135.

103
00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:27.920
And you'll notice as we do it,

104
00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:29.079
we get these little ticks here

105
00:04:29.079 --> 00:04:31.800
showing that the animation is looping,

106
00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:34.300
which is perfect, that's exactly what we want.

107
00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:38.420
All right, so the next step is that we need to

108
00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:39.579
kind of move this character

109
00:04:39.579 --> 00:04:41.480
to make it look like he's actually running.

110
00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:43.800
Now, we're going to do this completely by hand,

111
00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:45.480
there are other methods for this,

112
00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:47.740
but we're just gonna go super basic here.

113
00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:50.819
So under my SKM bot, underneath Transform,

114
00:04:50.819 --> 00:04:52.660
I'm gonna click the little plus button in the middle,

115
00:04:52.939 --> 00:04:55.420
which is add key to the current time.

116
00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:57.060
And then I'll go ahead and add a keyframe

117
00:04:57.060 --> 00:05:00.339
and record the position information of our character.

118
00:05:00.339 --> 00:05:03.019
And then you can see the little square that was created,

119
00:05:03.019 --> 00:05:06.800
that is a reference to our key, our timeline.

120
00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:09.439
All right, now before I move the character,

121
00:05:09.439 --> 00:05:11.279
if you remember, we have this option

122
00:05:11.279 --> 00:05:14.459
to create keyframes when a property changes.

123
00:05:14.459 --> 00:05:16.000
So we're gonna have that activated

124
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:18.259
just to make it easier on ourselves.

125
00:05:18.259 --> 00:05:21.600
I'll drag this out to our 135,

126
00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:23.759
and what I'm gonna do is activate my move,

127
00:05:23.759 --> 00:05:25.319
and I'm gonna move our character

128
00:05:25.319 --> 00:05:27.860
right to the end of the platform.

129
00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:29.240
Now you can see that it automatically

130
00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:30.639
created a new keyframe,

131
00:05:30.639 --> 00:05:33.920
we can see that displayed in our viewport as well.

132
00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:36.920
And if I go back to our start and play,

133
00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:38.240
see the character moving,

134
00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:40.920
and it's not bad, this is pretty good.

135
00:05:42.509 --> 00:05:43.689
One of the thing you may have noticed

136
00:05:43.689 --> 00:05:47.129
is it looks like he's kind of skating on ice at the start

137
00:05:47.129 --> 00:05:50.529
and the reason for that is our keyframe interpolation.

138
00:05:50.529 --> 00:05:52.050
For this shot, I want the character

139
00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:54.529
to look like he's already midway in running,

140
00:05:54.529 --> 00:05:56.769
and we just happen to open the frame,

141
00:05:56.769 --> 00:05:59.550
open the camera as he's running past.

142
00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:01.490
So to do that, I'm just going to select

143
00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:04.050
my first keyframe, right click on it,

144
00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:07.250
and change our key interpolation to linear.

145
00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:09.089
Now if we play, you'll see he kind of looks

146
00:06:09.089 --> 00:06:10.889
like he's already starting to run,

147
00:06:10.889 --> 00:06:13.740
and I'd say that's pretty good.

148
00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:17.129
All right, we could do the same thing here on this one

149
00:06:17.129 --> 00:06:19.269
if we wanted to, change it to linear,

150
00:06:19.269 --> 00:06:22.269
go back, and now we can see him running,

151
00:06:22.269 --> 00:06:24.829
and you see he kind of goes and stops.

152
00:06:24.829 --> 00:06:27.370
I think that works well for us right now.

153
00:06:27.370 --> 00:06:29.230
All right, perfect, so we have our animation,

154
00:06:29.230 --> 00:06:32.910
our run, and we have our transform.

155
00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:33.829
The next thing that we need to do

156
00:06:33.829 --> 00:06:37.250
is we need to have him jump through the air and fall.

157
00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:38.370
So the first thing I want to do

158
00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:41.250
is actually expand my timeline out here,

159
00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:43.449
because you notice that we stop at 150,

160
00:06:43.449 --> 00:06:46.490
and roughly the, I think we've got what,

161
00:06:46.490 --> 00:06:48.870
15 keyframes is not enough for the animation,

162
00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:52.430
so let's go ahead and extend our timeline out.

163
00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:53.670
So to do that, a couple ways,

164
00:06:53.670 --> 00:06:55.949
we could increase our view range,

165
00:06:55.949 --> 00:06:58.750
or I can simply go down here and type in,

166
00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:02.389
I'll do something like 200, it'll move our timeline,

167
00:07:02.389 --> 00:07:04.629
and I'm gonna click this little red arrow,

168
00:07:04.629 --> 00:07:07.310
which will then set the out point now to 200.

169
00:07:07.310 --> 00:07:10.100
Give us ourselves a little bit more time.

170
00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:13.100
I'm gonna move my play head back to 135,

171
00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:15.620
and we're going to add another animation.

172
00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:17.459
Go back down to our animation track,

173
00:07:17.459 --> 00:07:21.459
click our plus, and we are going to search for jump.

174
00:07:21.459 --> 00:07:25.360
We're looking for the Abot Run Jump.

175
00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:26.259
So we'll go ahead and add that,

176
00:07:26.259 --> 00:07:28.620
and now you'll see that as he runs,

177
00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:32.060
he'll then go to our jump animation, which is perfect,

178
00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:35.689
and I'll expand this out just a little bit.

179
00:07:35.689 --> 00:07:37.649
And then let's go ahead and just add the third animation,

180
00:07:37.649 --> 00:07:40.209
which is going to be our falling animation.

181
00:07:40.209 --> 00:07:42.250
So same thing, click our little plus,

182
00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:43.569
there it is at the top, fall,

183
00:07:43.569 --> 00:07:46.410
but you can search for fall, Abot Fall.

184
00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:49.129
And so now you'll see he runs, jumps,

185
00:07:49.129 --> 00:07:52.850
and then goes into his falling animation, all right?

186
00:07:52.850 --> 00:07:53.930
And let me go over here real fast

187
00:07:53.930 --> 00:07:56.209
and just show you something that's happening.

188
00:07:56.209 --> 00:08:00.610
So as you see, he jumps, and then he snaps to our fall,

189
00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:02.449
which is not what we want.

190
00:08:02.449 --> 00:08:05.050
So I can select my fall animation

191
00:08:05.050 --> 00:08:07.050
and actually drag this back,

192
00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:09.490
and you'll notice that it starts to blend.

193
00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:11.329
And what's gonna happen here is

194
00:08:11.329 --> 00:08:14.610
this animation is going to blend the keyframes,

195
00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:17.250
so the skeletal position of all of our joints,

196
00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:19.490
between the jump and the fall.

197
00:08:19.490 --> 00:08:23.149
So now we should get something that looks a little smoother.

198
00:08:23.149 --> 00:08:24.970
We do have a little hiccup there.

199
00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:27.509
We'll see as we animate it, what's going on,

200
00:08:27.509 --> 00:08:30.449
but this should get us within that range.

201
00:08:30.449 --> 00:08:32.929
In fact, actually, let's go ahead and fix that now.

202
00:08:32.929 --> 00:08:35.610
So I'm just gonna quickly undo this

203
00:08:35.610 --> 00:08:36.690
just to get back to my frames.

204
00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:38.570
And the reason why we have this little pop

205
00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:41.210
is you'll notice that the jump animation is only so long.

206
00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:43.610
So let's constrain the keyframe there,

207
00:08:43.610 --> 00:08:46.470
move our fall back here, and then blend.

208
00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:49.370
So this should make it a little easier, and there we go.

209
00:08:49.370 --> 00:08:51.730
Now we've got our smooth transition.

210
00:08:51.730 --> 00:08:53.809
So real quick, just to recap what happened there,

211
00:08:53.809 --> 00:08:58.009
our jump animation was really only meant to happen once,

212
00:08:58.009 --> 00:09:01.009
but because we had extended this animation

213
00:09:01.009 --> 00:09:03.250
to include a second loop,

214
00:09:03.250 --> 00:09:05.730
it just produced an interesting and weird look.

215
00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:06.690
So we just clipped it back

216
00:09:06.690 --> 00:09:09.610
to the actual animation timeline and blended,

217
00:09:09.610 --> 00:09:11.600
and now we should be good to go.

218
00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:13.240
All right, so at this juncture,

219
00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:15.480
we just need to animate the rest of his movement.

220
00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:17.919
So we do have our auto keyframe on,

221
00:09:17.919 --> 00:09:20.600
and I'm gonna go to about the apex here

222
00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:24.879
of this little jump here,

223
00:09:24.879 --> 00:09:27.759
and let's just move him up into the air.

224
00:09:27.759 --> 00:09:29.720
That looks about pretty good.

225
00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:32.759
Now, if you'll notice, something happened here.

226
00:09:32.759 --> 00:09:34.919
So this is the keyframes,

227
00:09:34.919 --> 00:09:36.480
and what's gonna happen is he's gonna run,

228
00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:38.759
but he's going vertical.

229
00:09:38.759 --> 00:09:40.100
And the reason for that

230
00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:42.039
is because our auto keyframe is turned on,

231
00:09:42.039 --> 00:09:45.559
it's only going to add a key when the property changes.

232
00:09:45.559 --> 00:09:48.039
So if I expand our transform out,

233
00:09:48.039 --> 00:09:50.399
and in particular, our location,

234
00:09:50.399 --> 00:09:54.000
you'll notice that when I manually created a keyframe,

235
00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:55.919
it created a keyframe for all of our properties.

236
00:09:55.919 --> 00:09:58.879
So location, all the channels, rotation and scale.

237
00:09:58.879 --> 00:10:02.539
But when we made our adjustment at 135,

238
00:10:02.539 --> 00:10:06.659
only the X and Y position changed, his Z did not.

239
00:10:06.659 --> 00:10:10.120
So when we did our jump, we of course changed Z,

240
00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:12.139
and it says, oh, I'm gonna add a keyframe.

241
00:10:12.139 --> 00:10:14.600
So we can do this several different ways,

242
00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:17.000
but this will be the cleanest and most efficient.

243
00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:18.480
So I'm gonna go back to our 135,

244
00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:19.799
and I'm gonna go to my Z channel

245
00:10:19.799 --> 00:10:24.740
and just create a new keyframe just for that channel.

246
00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:26.759
Now, if we go back, we were going,

247
00:10:26.759 --> 00:10:30.509
let's go ahead and move him upwards as if he's jumping.

248
00:10:30.509 --> 00:10:33.470
And you'll notice now he goes through his cycle runs

249
00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:36.029
and then jumps, just perfect.

250
00:10:36.029 --> 00:10:38.710
Then let's go to the end of our fall here

251
00:10:38.710 --> 00:10:42.269
and just move him down to something that feels right.

252
00:10:42.269 --> 00:10:45.289
He'll be off camera, so not super concerned.

253
00:10:45.289 --> 00:10:47.000
All right.

254
00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:49.279
Now, if you've worked with animation before,

255
